WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.987
[MUSIC]

00:00:04.987 --> 00:00:10.030
There years ago I went on
my first date with Brian.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:13.248
We had matched on a dating app and
started texting right away.

00:00:13.248 --> 00:00:16.840
He was so enthusiastic and compassionate.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.410
He would text me hey,
with an exclamation point.

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.169
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:21.169 --> 00:00:23.374
&gt;&gt; Followed by how's it going?

00:00:23.374 --> 00:00:24.674
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:24.674 --> 00:00:26.115
&gt;&gt; We really hit it off.

00:00:26.115 --> 00:00:29.120
He was just so easy to talk to.

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:31.755
Fast forward to our first date.

00:00:31.755 --> 00:00:34.808
It was aweful.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:34.808 --> 00:00:36.855
&gt;&gt; We couldn't keep a conversation going,

00:00:36.855 --> 00:00:39.844
his voice was monotone,
he failed to make eye make contact.

00:00:39.844 --> 00:00:46.029
And he wasn't even asking questions, so
much for enthusiastic and compassionate.

00:00:46.029 --> 00:00:48.630
Frustration set in as I counted
down the minutes until freedom.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:56.380
This situation is a prime example of
what I call fictionalizing reality.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:01:02.144
My conversations with Brian were real but
they were just texts on a screen.

00:01:02.144 --> 00:01:05.710
No body language, no vocal cues.

00:01:05.710 --> 00:01:07.938
I was working with an incomplete picture.

00:01:07.938 --> 00:01:12.030
So to compensate I colored in what was
missing with bright shades of exuberance

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:12.650
and charm.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:15.128
I had fictionalized my reality.

00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:19.343
Technology had brought me to Brian.

00:01:19.343 --> 00:01:23.859
But it also introduced ambiguity,
which ultimately led to my disappointment.

00:01:25.558 --> 00:01:29.233
We are more digitally
connected than ever before,

00:01:29.233 --> 00:01:34.026
exchanging on average 250 text
messages and emails every day.

00:01:34.026 --> 00:01:37.515
But only so
much can be said with a digital message,

00:01:37.515 --> 00:01:40.203
which leads to a number of challenges.

00:01:40.203 --> 00:01:42.338
In his research on human communication,

00:01:42.338 --> 00:01:46.200
Professor Albert Murabian hints
toward some of these challenges.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:50.088
And why technology may interfere
with three critical elements of our

00:01:50.088 --> 00:01:51.032
conversation.

00:01:51.032 --> 00:01:54.860
Our clarity, our intention and
our vulnerability.

00:01:55.990 --> 00:02:00.097
He explains that our non-verbal cues,
specifically our tone of voice and

00:02:00.097 --> 00:02:01.016
body language.

00:02:01.016 --> 00:02:04.147
Help change the attitudes and
feelings of others more so

00:02:04.147 --> 00:02:05.820
than the actual words we say.

00:02:06.830 --> 00:02:11.503
Let me give you an example,
I could say, I can't wait or

00:02:11.503 --> 00:02:14.728
I can't wait.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:14.728 --> 00:02:17.239
&gt;&gt; My tone of voice and body language help

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:21.562
to communicate my excitement and
enthusiasm or, lack there of.

00:02:21.562 --> 00:02:26.794
He further explains that 93% of our
impact is attributable to these

00:02:26.794 --> 00:02:32.060
non verbal cues whereas our actual
content contributes to just 7%.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.432
This concept also applies to
text messaging and email.

00:02:36.432 --> 00:02:43.310
Our message communicates 7% of our message
but leaving us in the 93% darkness.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:48.356
Where it's hard to detect clarity,
intention, and vulnerability.

00:02:48.356 --> 00:02:53.370
As recipients, we're often left
feeling confused or doubtful.

00:02:53.370 --> 00:02:55.855
As senders, anxious and misunderstood.

00:02:57.490 --> 00:03:02.456
Both sides are left feeling disconnected,
but why?

00:03:02.456 --> 00:03:05.902
First, technology compromises clarity.

00:03:05.902 --> 00:03:08.373
Last week I had to cancel
plans with my friend.

00:03:08.373 --> 00:03:12.870
I sent him a quick text explaining
the situation, expecting disappointment.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.861
A few hours later my phone buzzed,
his response,

00:03:18.861 --> 00:03:21.702
it's okay, I understand.

00:03:21.702 --> 00:03:22.614
It's okay.

00:03:22.614 --> 00:03:24.730
I understand.

00:03:24.730 --> 00:03:25.510
[SOUND] He was definitely mad.

00:03:25.510 --> 00:03:27.187
It's okay.

00:03:27.187 --> 00:03:29.510
I understand.

00:03:29.510 --> 00:03:31.770
Totally fine.

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.156
But which was it?

00:03:33.156 --> 00:03:36.105
He hadn't used an emoji or
an exclamation point.

00:03:36.105 --> 00:03:36.922
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:36.922 --> 00:03:38.127
&gt;&gt; He must be mad.

00:03:38.127 --> 00:03:40.010
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:03:40.010 --> 00:03:42.050
&gt;&gt; This conversation had me doubting

00:03:42.050 --> 00:03:45.997
the status of our friendship,
I was embarrassed by my flakiness.

00:03:45.997 --> 00:03:50.562
I was afraid to reach out,
it was unclear what he was saying.

00:03:50.562 --> 00:03:53.442
I decided I may be
overthinking everything, so

00:03:53.442 --> 00:03:56.050
I decided to give him a call.

00:03:56.050 --> 00:04:00.901
Sure enough he did understand, and we
re-scheduled our dinner for another night.

00:04:00.901 --> 00:04:04.090
I had fictionalized
the 93% of his message.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:05.410
He had only given me 7%.

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:10.210
And unfortunately for us,
as psychology research shows,

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:14.823
when we're given ambiguity
we tend to assume the worst.

00:04:14.823 --> 00:04:19.741
I'm sure, as you have experienced,
even the smallest language or punctuation

00:04:19.741 --> 00:04:24.610
choice can lead to hours, days, or
even weeks of frustration and resentment.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:30.095
Secondly, technology
compromises intention.

00:04:30.095 --> 00:04:32.455
Prior to starting my MBA
program at Standford,

00:04:32.455 --> 00:04:35.052
I was a young business
professional on Wall Street.

00:04:35.052 --> 00:04:38.605
Making PowerPoints and consuming
caffeine like my job depended on it.

00:04:38.605 --> 00:04:39.349
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:39.349 --> 00:04:41.523
&gt;&gt; To be clear, my job did depend on it.

00:04:41.523 --> 00:04:43.229
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:43.229 --> 00:04:45.466
&gt;&gt; We worked tirelessly on an extensive

00:04:45.466 --> 00:04:46.264
pitch deck.

00:04:46.264 --> 00:04:51.470
I was on version 16 when I sent the
document to my boss for a final review.

00:04:51.470 --> 00:04:54.120
The next morning,
an email sat at the top of my inbox.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:57.665
I took a deep breath and
clicked into the email, thx.

00:04:57.665 --> 00:04:59.756
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:04:59.756 --> 00:05:04.010
&gt;&gt; T-H-X period.

00:05:04.010 --> 00:05:05.160
What?

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:06.595
Are you kidding me?
Did I make a mistake?

00:05:06.595 --> 00:05:08.994
Does he know how long I spent
working on this project?

00:05:08.994 --> 00:05:14.001
There is no way I am spending
this much time on the next one.

00:05:14.001 --> 00:05:17.630
N-O W-A-Y, period.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:05:17.630 --> 00:05:19.493
&gt;&gt; This email from my boss left me

00:05:19.493 --> 00:05:22.186
feeling unappreciated and unsupported.

00:05:22.186 --> 00:05:24.866
This interaction wasn't uncommon with him,
but

00:05:24.866 --> 00:05:28.480
this last message left me
feeling completely demoralized.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:31.794
To the point that I considered looking for
a new role,

00:05:31.794 --> 00:05:36.446
that is until I ran into my boss, and
he congratulated me on a job well done.

00:05:36.446 --> 00:05:37.794
Wait, what?

00:05:37.794 --> 00:05:41.571
To my surprise, he had been so
incredibly impressed with the pitch deck.

00:05:41.571 --> 00:05:46.880
That he printed the deck as is,
replied with a quick THX, and moved on.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:51.657
Failing to see the intention
behind his thanks,

00:05:51.657 --> 00:05:54.565
I had fictionalized my reality yet again.

00:05:54.565 --> 00:05:58.616
When it comes to email and texts we
are often overconfident in our ability to

00:05:58.616 --> 00:06:01.490
convey our complete thoughts and feelings.

00:06:01.490 --> 00:06:04.814
Assuming that the recipient shares
the same context that we do.

00:06:04.814 --> 00:06:09.202
This can lead to problems, reducing
self-confidence and morale over time,

00:06:09.202 --> 00:06:12.333
which can deteriorate your
important relationships.

00:06:14.445 --> 00:06:18.050
Lastly, technology
compromises vulnerability.

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:22.521
My phone buzzed as I
received one text message.

00:06:22.521 --> 00:06:28.700
Just two words,
My body fell limp in my seat.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:33.724
My dad had informed me that my
only grandfather had passed away.

00:06:33.724 --> 00:06:41.180
But his message felt abrupt,
harsh, emotionless.

00:06:41.180 --> 00:06:44.752
What I sought in this moment was
support and strength from my dad.

00:06:44.752 --> 00:06:47.440
But instead, I felt disconnected.

00:06:49.020 --> 00:06:52.220
I was afraid to delve
into the 93% darkness.

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.094
I couldn't fictionalize this reality.

00:06:59.220 --> 00:07:01.641
I failed to detect my
father's true feelings.

00:07:01.641 --> 00:07:05.165
And I struggled to expose my
own vulnerability to him.

00:07:05.165 --> 00:07:11.618
Again I've been provided a snippet of
content but the rest was ambiguous.

00:07:11.618 --> 00:07:15.160
It's hard to choose the right words for
a text to email.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:18.698
Making it hard to express our true
feelings in important moments like

00:07:18.698 --> 00:07:19.247
this one.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:25.358
Technology allows us to
accomplish incredible things.

00:07:25.358 --> 00:07:29.868
But by virtue of its digital form,
it can get in the way of truth and

00:07:29.868 --> 00:07:31.429
genuine connection.

00:07:31.429 --> 00:07:36.352
So if you seek to build meaningful
connection, how exactly do you do that?

00:07:36.352 --> 00:07:38.918
Strive for clarity.

00:07:38.918 --> 00:07:42.310
Instead of it's okay, I understand.

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:47.944
Try it's okay, thanks for letting me know,
I'd love to catch up, let's re-schedule.

00:07:47.944 --> 00:07:49.980
A clear tone goes a long way.

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.452
Express your intention explicitly.

00:07:54.452 --> 00:07:57.010
Instead of replying with, thanks.

00:07:57.010 --> 00:08:01.303
You could try, this is great,
I appreciate your hard work, thanks.

00:08:01.303 --> 00:08:05.572
With just eight additional words my
boss could've adequately expressed his

00:08:05.572 --> 00:08:08.340
appreciation, giving me
confidence in my work.

00:08:09.860 --> 00:08:13.200
Convey more emotion even when difficult.

00:08:14.450 --> 00:08:18.770
Your grandfather has passed away,
I know this is hard for all of us, but

00:08:18.770 --> 00:08:21.511
stay strong,
call me when you can I love you.

00:08:24.490 --> 00:08:28.930
Digital messaging is a powerful tool and
if you use it with intention,

00:08:28.930 --> 00:08:31.044
you can wield powerful results.

00:08:31.044 --> 00:08:34.876
You can prevent others from
fictionalizing their reality.

00:08:34.876 --> 00:08:38.386
Instead of creating
unnecessary frustration or

00:08:38.386 --> 00:08:42.330
distance, you can build
self confidence and morale.

00:08:43.510 --> 00:08:46.900
Join me in tackling the dark
side of digital communication.

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:54.814
So we can build better relationships.
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:08:54.814 --> 00:08:58.761
[MUSIC]

